# 7. Version Control

All configuration items shall be stored in Git.

Versioning rules:

* Documentation → Semantic Versioning.
* Source Code → Git commit history and release tags.
* Database → Sequential migration versions.
* Releases → Semantic Versioning.

Example release tags:

```text id="jvow4w"
v1.0.0

v1.1.0

v1.1.1

v2.0.0
```

---

# 8. Branching Strategy

The project shall use the following Git branching model.

```text id="vq5jlwm"
main

develop

feature/*

release/*

hotfix/*
```

Branch purposes:

| Branch    | Purpose                   |
| --------- | ------------------------- |
| main      | Production-ready baseline |
| develop   | Integration branch        |
| feature/* | New functionality         |
| release/* | Release preparation       |
| hotfix/*  | Urgent production fixes   |

Direct commits to `main` are prohibited.

---

# 9. Baselines

The following baselines shall be maintained.

| Baseline               | Description                             |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Documentation Baseline | Approved PML and architecture documents |
| Development Baseline   | Stable integration build                |
| Testing Baseline       | Approved test suite                     |
| Release Baseline       | Production release                      |
| Operational Baseline   | Live production environment             |

Each baseline shall be reproducible.

---

# 10. Configuration Change Process

All configuration changes shall follow this workflow.

```text id="grxwpv"
Change Request
      │
      ▼
Impact Assessment
      │
      ▼
Approval
      │
      ▼
Implementation
      │
      ▼
Testing
      │
      ▼
Review
      │
      ▼
Merge
      │
      ▼
Baseline Update
```

Every change shall be recorded through Git history.

---

# 11. Build Reproducibility

Every approved release shall be reproducible from:

* Tagged source code.
* Approved configuration files.
* Approved database migrations.
* Approved dependencies.
* Approved build scripts.

No manual modifications shall be required after checkout.

---

# 12. Release Identification

Every production release shall include:

* Version number
* Release tag
* Build number
* Commit hash
* Release date
* Change log
* Supporting documentation

Each release shall be uniquely identifiable.

---

# 13. Environment Configuration

The following environments shall be maintained separately.

| Environment | Purpose                     |
| ----------- | --------------------------- |
| Development | Active development          |
| Integration | Feature integration         |
| Testing     | Verification and validation |
| Staging     | Pre-production validation   |
| Production  | Live operations             |

Configuration values shall be externalised and environment-specific.

---

# 14. Audit & Traceability

Configuration Management shall support complete traceability between:

* Requirements
* PML Standards
* ADRs
* Architecture
* Source Code
* Test Cases
* Releases

This enables end-to-end lifecycle auditing.

---

# 15. Backup & Recovery

The project repository shall be protected through:

* Remote Git hosting.
* Regular repository backups.
* Database backups.
* Release archive retention.
* Disaster recovery procedures.

Repository integrity shall be periodically verified.

---

# 16. Responsibilities

| Role               | Responsibilities                |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------- |
| Solution Architect | Configuration governance        |
| Technical Lead     | Source code baseline            |
| Developers         | Correct branching and commits   |
| DevOps Engineer    | Build and release configuration |
| Database Engineer  | Database version management     |
| QA Engineer        | Test baseline verification      |

---

# 17. Compliance

All contributors shall:

* Use Git for version control.
* Follow the approved branching strategy.
* Reference configuration changes in commits.
* Maintain reproducible builds.
* Protect production baselines.
* Follow the Project Master Library.

Configuration items outside these controls shall not be considered part of the official Trading Intelligence Platform.

---

# 18. Configuration Management Statement

Configuration Management provides the controls required to ensure that every software component, document, database object, configuration file, and release within the Trading Intelligence Platform remains consistent, traceable, reproducible, and auditable throughout the project's lifecycle.

